<!--
  One version for the whole product, defined once.

  There are three executables in a bundle and they must agree. The WinUI 3 build
  used to carry its own copy of the number and the native launcher read it from
  there, which is how a v1.1.2 window ended up inside a v1.2.0 bundle. Adding a
  third front-end made the drift visible rather than fixing it: Avalonia
  declared 1.0.0 while WinUI declared 1.2.1, in the same folder.

  A Directory.Build.props at src/ is the only place MSBuild will pick up
  automatically for every project beneath it, so both .csproj files now take
  their version from here. Bump this one file.

  The native launcher has no project file, so scripts/build-launcher.ps1 parses
  the <Version> element out of this file. If you move or rename it, update that
  script too. It fails loudly rather than falling back to a wrong number.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>1.2.2</Version>
    <AssemblyVersion>1.2.2.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>1.2.2.0</FileVersion>
    <InformationalVersion>1.2.2</InformationalVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
